KG42.register("rules-60514b0309de0f901fff421e",{"id":"41-data_center_local_markets::v141-c-R005","paper":"41-data_center_local_markets","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R005","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Consumer participation","process_stage":"participation","market_meaning":"Local energy-market participants may act as energy buyers at some time points to purchase deficit energy. Scope: Background description of participant roles in local energy markets. The CSP can purchase deficit energy from other end energy prosumers through the local energy markets that host its IDCs. Scope: The CSP's participation in the multiple local energy markets that host its networked IDCs. Condition: Through scheduling computing requests in the IDC network and controlling power outputs of the on-site energy resources. The CSP's energy purchases are restricted to the grid; local-market buyer participation is unavailable in this benchmark case. Scope: Benchmark Case 2 in the CSP model comparison. Condition: Without the local energy trading mechanism. The CSP can still purchase energy from local energy markets in this benchmark case. Scope: Benchmark Case 3 in the CSP model comparison. Condition: Without BESSs and renewable energy sources.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function participation.consumer under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["participation.consumer"],"E":[],"omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Background description of participant roles in local energy markets. | Benchmark Case 2 in the CSP model comparison. | Benchmark Case 3 in the CSP model comparison. | The CSP's participation in the multiple local energy markets that host its networked IDCs.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E02","v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g02-E19"]},"x":[],"theta":[],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Local energy-market participants may act as energy buyers at some time points to purchase deficit energy. Scope: Background description of participant roles in local energy markets.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E02"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The CSP can purchase deficit energy from other end energy prosumers through the local energy markets that host its IDCs. Scope: The CSP's participation in the multiple local energy markets that host its networked IDCs. Condition: Through scheduling computing requests in the IDC network and controlling power outputs of the on-site energy resources.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E04"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The CSP's energy purchases are restricted to the grid; local-market buyer participation is unavailable in this benchmark case. Scope: Benchmark Case 2 in the CSP model comparison. Condition: Without the local energy trading mechanism.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E19"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The CSP can still purchase energy from local energy markets in this benchmark case. Scope: Benchmark Case 3 in the CSP model comparison. Condition: Without BESSs and renewable energy sources.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E19"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E02","location":{"location_id":"src-307a684414-p02-c1r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"Nomenclature / Introduction","anchor_text":"more practical scenario would be enabling IDCs to participate in local energy markets in power distr","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Unlike grid-scale energy markets, the participants in a local energy market can have dual roles: in some time points, it can act as an energy buyer to purchase deficit energy from the market; in some other time points, it can act as an energy seller to sell the surplus energy to the market. Both auction market-based and Peer-to-Peer (P2P) negotiation-based mechanisms have been studied for the local energy trading. In the former, energy consumers and producers submit energy purchase/selling bids to an auction-based marketplace, and the market operator stacks the bids and determines the market clearing price for energy trading. In the latter, energy consumers and producers autonomously negotiate the price and amount of energy trading in a P2P manner [11]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-307a684414-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.1 System overview","anchor_text":"a ir In this study, we consider a CSP that manages K networked IDCs. The gy IDCs are geographically ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In this study, we consider a CSP that manages K networked IDCs. The IDCs are geographically distributed in K different areas, where there is a local energy market in each area. The K networked IDCs can receive end users’ computing requests delivered by W third-party communication infrastructure providers. Each IDC is considered to be equipped with the following on-site IT and energy resources: (i) a number of servers that process computing requests; (ii) RESs, including photovoltaic solar panels and/or wind turbines; and (iii) a BESS that is used to accommodate the on-site renewable energy and provide energy backup support to the IDC. Through properly scheduling computing requests in the IDC network and controlling power outputs of the on-site energy resources, the CSP can alter the temporal-spatial power consumption and production distribution of the IDC network. This, in turn, allows the CSP to purchase deficit energy from and sell surplus energy to other end energy prosumers (e.g. buildings and electric vehicles) via multiple local energy markets that host the IDCs. Such vision is depicted in Fig. 1."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-307a684414-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"5.2 Energy management results","anchor_text":"each IDC s connected power line is set at 500 kW. All programs are implemented by Matlab and execute","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"For validation purpose, we compare the proposed energy management model with 3 benchmark cases:\n• Case 1 – the proposed model;\n• Case 2 – without local energy trading mechanism. That is, the CSP can only purchase energy from the grid, while the surplus energy generated by the IDCs will be fed back into the grid without subsidy;\n• Case 3 – without BESSs and renewable energy sources. In this case, the CSP can still purchase energy from both the grid and the local energy markets, but it cannot sell energy to the markets due to the absence of these facilities.\n• Case 4 – without temporal scheduling of computing requests. That is, each request is allocated to an IDC and is immediately processed in its arrival time slot."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":"C explicitly supplied only source-backed unary facts for this function; other graph gaps remain reviewable.","empty_x_reason":"No decision or design-selection variable is defined for participation.consumer in this frozen rule or in its supplied B evidence. The CSP request-scheduling, BESS-operation, and price-proposal variables appear under other rules and cannot be moved into this consumer-participation rule without changing its scope. This empty list does not assert that the paper lacks variables.","empty_theta_reason":"No fixed coefficient, price, capacity, elasticity, time horizon, or other parameter is attached to participation.consumer by this rule's B evidence; the numerical case-study settings support other rules. This empty list does not assert that the paper lacks parameters."},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-clearing","v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-participation","v141-a-M01-pricing"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E02","quote":"the participants in a local energy market can have dual roles: in some time points, it can act as an energy buyer to purchase deficit energy from the market"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","quote":"This, in turn, allows the CSP to purchase deficit energy from and sell surplus energy to other end energy prosumers (e.g. buildings and electric vehicles) via multiple local energy markets that host the IDCs."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E19","quote":"Case 2 – without local energy trading mechanism. That is, the CSP can only purchase energy from the grid"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E19","quote":"Case 3 – without BESSs and renewable energy sources. In this case, the CSP can still purchase energy from both the grid and the local energy markets"}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"1ee3211731cf65cef654a57065ab0a1acf3c10ad4277de10f34af829d57b1856","applicability":"non_mathematical","reason":"The frozen rule contains only the unary market-role object participation.consumer and procedural source statements: local-market participants may be buyers at some times, the CSP may purchase deficit energy through the local markets hosting its IDCs, and the benchmark cases specify when local-market buyer participation is unavailable (Case 2) or remains possible (Case 3). The supplied B evidence provides no equation, variable, parameter, threshold, index domain, or quantifier defining consumer participation itself. The source's quantitative buy/sell price and net-load conditions belong to other rules and graph objects not present in this frozen rule, so importing them here would change the rule's meaning. The rule is therefore recorded as non-mathematical/procedural with all original C.1 facts preserved; this is not a claim that the paper contains no mathematics. B evidence remains unreviewed in this experiment.","bindings":[],"revision_requests":[]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
